# a-wave-approaches — the first script in dryopea's history that
# asserts the GAME works rather than that a function returns.
#
# A core at the origin, a spawn twelve hexes east of it facing back
# the way it came, and a wave of six.  Then the thing that matters:
# the enemies exist, they are alive, and the distance between them
# and the core goes DOWN.
#
# Twelve hexes out is deliberate — a spawn inside
# close_spawn_disable_radius (10) is silenced, and a wave that
# emitted nothing would let every `count alive` below pass for the
# wrong reason.
#
# The corridor of grass is not decoration either; see the drag below.

echo a wave approaches: six enemies walking at the core

do toggle_mode

# The core is where the target is.
do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

# A spawn facing direction 4 — north-west, which from due east of
# the core is the way back towards it.
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 12 0
marker 12 0 spawn 4

count markers 2 2
do toggle_mode

# The ground they walk on.  Plan 11 F1b made the walk consult the
# world — an enemy stops at what its class cannot cross — and an
# UNPAINTED hex is sea, so before F1b these six were walking on
# water and after it they would not move at all.  One drag paints
# the corridor from the spawn to the core.
palette 5
drag 12 0 0 0
count painted 13 13

wave 6
count alive 6 6
range 12 12
snap wave-starts

tick 4
count alive 6 6
range 8 8

tick 4
count alive 6 6
range 4 4
snap wave-closes
